  "worked_solution": "Since we are given that the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature: \n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and evaluate it at $s={\\pi }^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{s}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{s}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\sqrt{3})⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{s}}{2\\sqrt{s}},\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{s}}{2\\sqrt{s}},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{s}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{s},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{s},\\,0⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}({\\pi }^{2}) & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\pi }⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\pi }⟨0,\\,-1,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we obtain the curvature:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ({\\pi }^{2}) & =‖{T}^{′}({\\pi }^{2})‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\cdot \\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$",
